WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates - Week 4 Student Video
Unit 1, Lesson 4: Logic

1
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.380
When you think of logic, who comes to mind?

2
00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.300
Probably somebody like Sherlock Holmes, who uses his wit and mind to

3
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:17.040
outsmart his opponents.

4
00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.040
Maybe you feel this way when you've had a conversation with somebody with a

5
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:31.520
different belief system or when you're sharing your faith.

6
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.760
It's as if it's this jockeying back and forth, trying to outsmart each other with

7
00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:41.720
logic. Well, this can certainly happen, but technically, logic is just the careful

8
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:47.640
and proper use of reason. Logic is important for Christians so we can arrive

9
00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.860
at truthful beliefs ourselves, but it's also important in conversations with other

10
00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.780
people so we can help them arrive at truth in a loving manner.

11
00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.640
Let me share a story that might help.

12
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:06.640
Some time ago when I was a graduate student studying philosophy, I had a memorable

13
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.100
conversation with a professor from an Ivy League school.

14
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.940
He said to me, he goes, oh, I think it's great that you're studying philosophy as a

15
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:19.460
Christian. Maybe you'd want to study Buddhist philosophy because all religions are

16
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.760
true. Now that struck me as very odd.

17
00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:24.700
I thought for a minute, wait a minute, how could all religions be true when they

18
00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:29.720
contradict on such fundamental teachings about God, the afterlife, and how you get

19
00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.020
salvation? So I simply asked him a question.

20
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.900
I said, do you mind if I ask you a question?

21
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.340
How could all religions be true?

22
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:41.600
Namely, Islam and Christianity. Muslims say if you believe Jesus is God, you've

23
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.200
committed the sin of shirk and you're certainly going to hell.

24
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:49.760
I said, Christians say you can't get to heaven unless you believe Jesus is God.

25
00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:54.700
How can both of these be true? He kind of fumbled around a little bit.

26
00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.060
We kept talking. Eventually he looked at me and he said, here's the deal.

27
00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.760
He said, religion aside, what really is true is cultural relativism.

28
00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.200
Therefore, you should be true. You should be tolerant.

29
00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:12.000
Now stop and think about this. Use your mind, your logic to analyze this claim.

30
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.740
He said he believed in cultural relativism, which says morality and truth depends

31
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:23.120
upon the culture. But then he turned to me and said, therefore, because

32
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.480
morality is relative, I should be tolerant.

33
00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.660
Now do you see the inconsistency?

34
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:35.740
If morality is relative, he can't turn to me and say, I ought to be tolerant because

35
00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:40.180
he's appealing to tolerance as if it's a universal truth claim that everybody should

36
00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:45.360
follow. On the flip side, he says, if he says that everybody should be tolerant,

37
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:50.520
then he doesn't actually believe that morality is relative because he's appealing to

38
00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.840
the universal principle or standard of tolerance.

39
00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.600
Friends, people hold a lot of different views about God, religion and morality.

40
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:02.740
And one important thing to do is to listen to their views and draw it out.

41
00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:06.560
And what we'll often see is when we do that, their views will lead to inconsistency

42
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:09.560
and sometimes even to logical absurdity.

43
00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:15.920
For example, in Matthew 22:41 through 45, Jesus uses the

44
00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:20.440
known logical principle of reductio ad absurdum.

45
00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.320
Now that sounds really fancy, but essentially what it means is reducing a position

46
00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:30.960
to absurdity. In this passage in Matthew, Jesus is having a conversation with the

47
00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.480
Pharisees. And he asks them, who do you think the Messiah is?

48
00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.720
Is the Messiah the son of David?

49
00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.360
And they say, yes, the Messiah is the son of David.

50
00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:45.100
And what Jesus says is meant to draw out their view to show that it leads to

51
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.840
absurdity. Now, how does he do this?

52
00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:52.540
He quotes from Psalms 110:1, in which David, who wrote that Psalm, says

53
00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:57.800
that the Messiah is his Lord. And Jesus says, wait a minute, you just said that the

54
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:03.180
Messiah is the son of David, but now we have David calling the Messiah Lord.

55
00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.820
How can that be? Now, what is Jesus' point?

56
00:04:06.080 --> 00:04:10.880
He's saying, if you think that the Messiah is merely the son of David, then that

57
00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:15.600
leads to absurdity. But rather, it's more reasonable to conclude that, yes, the

58
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.200
Messiah is the son of David, but he's more than human.

59
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:25.060
He actually is Lord, as David himself said in Psalms 110, verse

60
00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.180
1. In my conversation with an Ivy League professor, I was trying to show that he

61
00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:35.120
held two views in contradiction, that when drawn to their logical conclusion, led to

62
00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:39.680
absurdity. This is exactly what Jesus was doing with the Pharisees.

63
00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:45.960
This is why the Apostle Paul, in Ephesians 4, verses 14 through 15, says we are not

64
00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.520
to be like children in terms of our thinking, tossed to and fro by different waves

65
00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.640
of doctrine. Rather, we are to grow up and be mature in the way we think.

66
00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:02.020
Paul also says in this passage that we are to speak truth in love.

67
00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:05.080
In other words, we are called to speak truth.

68
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:09.320
We can't compromise truth. We don't speak truth just to win arguments.

69
00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:12.080
We don't speak truth just to prove people wrong.

70
00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:17.560
We do it, but because we know that it's only truth that can set people

71
00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:23.380
free. And please keep in mind, your conversations will not always go as

72
00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:26.520
smoothly as it seemed to go with me and the Ivy League professor.

73
00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:31.080
In fact, more often than not, after a conversation I have with somebody, I'll step

74
00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.900
away and go, oh, I should have said this.

75
00:05:32.920 --> 00:05:38.500
Oh, next time, I'll say that. Learning to love God with our minds and use logic is a

76
00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:44.500
lifelong process. You're learning some powerful stuff about truth and defending

77
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:47.060
the faith in this entire study that we're going through.

78
00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:50.200
It's easy to start thinking, great, I'm going to write this down, I'm going to go

79
00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:54.080
online, I'm going to go in person to my parents, my friends, other nonbelievers, and

80
00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.020
win some arguments. If that's what you're thinking, you're missing the point.

81
00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.980
The point of this is not to go out and win arguments.

82
00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:07.160
The point of this is, number one, to know truth for ourselves, and then second, to

83
00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:12.520
be able to speak that truth in love, to use reason, to use logic, to love

84
00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:17.780
people. That way, as people draw closer to you, learn more about your faith, they

85
00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:22.000
find something genuine and real that is not easily put out.

